  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nStrong monsoon moisture remains in place across the area with \nmany locations showing dew points well into the 50s early this \nmorning. A weak shortwave will work through the area this \nafternoon. This disturbance is not nearly as strong as the \nsystem that worked through yesterday, but still enough to allow \nnumerous showers along with isolated to scattered thunderstorms \nto develop. The models are really keying on the mountains and \nhighlands for development today, but there will probably still \nbe at least some isolated to scattered activity across the Magic\nValley into the Snake River Plain this afternoon. Locally heavy\nrain is a concern with these storms and we have targeted the \ntwo burn scars that have the best chances for debris flows with \na proven track record...Wapiti and Big Rock. Most but not all of\nthe showers will dissipate with the loss of the daytime heating\nthis evening.\n\nAnother round of numerous showers with isolated to scattered \nthunderstorms is expected Saturday as the remnants of the west coast \ntrough lift through our area. Models once again highlighting the \ncentral mountains for the best precip chances. Activity looks more \nscattered elsewhere as the pattern is starting to shift from \nsouthwest to west and the circulations helping to drive convection \nare closer to the mountains than east Idaho. Deep monsoon moisture \nis still around so given the amount of storms expected Saturday \nacross the central mountains and the heavy rain threat, have \ncontinued the flash flood watch for the Wapiti burn scar. Confidence \nis lower in thunderstorm coverage for Big Rock at this time. \n\nIsolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms will continue into \nSunday and Monday, but these should be mainly the heat of the day \nvariety pop up storms. Still plenty of moisture in place so \nunderneath the core of these storms will probably be some heavy \nrain. As we move into Tuesday and Wednesday, a trough slides through \nfrom the northwest. This may help with chances across the mountains \ninto the Island Park region. Looks like at least some slightly drier \nair behind the front for late next week.\n\nTemperatures expected to be well below normal once again today \nbecause of the cloud cover and showers. Temps gradually warm up into \nearly next week, but may not approach normal until late next week.",
